# Barn swallow

The barn swallow is the most widespread swallow in the world, glossy blue above
with a long forked tail. It builds an open cup nest from mud pellets and stalks
of dry grass, plastered to a rafter or a ledge inside a barn or under a bridge,
and lines it with feathers. A pair will build a new nest beside the old one or
repair the old one and use it again. Both adults feed the brood on insects
caught in flight, and European birds winter in southern Africa. See
[[hirundinidae]].
